If you're aiming for NEET PG 2027, one headline probably stopped your scroll this month: NEET PG is going rank-based. The news spread fast on X. One post on 4 November announcing the shift had more than 190,000 views and about 2,400 likes by 20 November (Indian Tech & Infra, 2026).

Before you rebuild your plan around it, note one word the viral post skipped. This is a **proposal**. It matters and it's moving, but it isn't a rule yet.

{% statCallout number="63,177" label="PG seats for 265,980 NEET-PG 2026 candidates, the pool every rank is drawn from (Hindustan Times, 2026)." /%}

## What was actually proposed

A 12-member expert committee, set up by the Union health ministry on 23 July 2026 and headed by the Director General of Health Services, filed its report in the Supreme Court through an affidavit. The court heard it on 3 November (LiveLaw, 2026; Hindustan Times, 4 Nov 2026).

The affidavit says NMC is preparing regulations under which, in its words, "only rank-based system for NEET-PG and NEET-SS counselling will be adopted, no percentile marks will be declared in results, only ranks will be given to candidates" (Hindustan Times, 4 Nov 2026).

Today, candidates must reach a minimum percentile to become eligible for counselling, and authorities can lower that percentile when seats stay empty. Under the proposal, candidates would be ranked and admitted by rank, instead of the qualifying percentile being lowered (Hindustan Times, 6 Nov 2026).

## Proposed, approved in principle, still pending

Headlines keep blending three different stages together, so here they are one at a time.

{% table %}
- **Step**
- **Status**
---
- **Expert panel recommendation**
- Made. Filed in the Supreme Court by the Centre on 3 Nov 2026.
---
- **NMC approval**
- In principle only. Reported as approved at NMC's 23 Sept meeting, communicated to the DGHS by letter on 1 Oct (Times of India, 2026).
---
- **Draft regulations**
- Not yet issued. A draft is expected after stakeholder comments (LiveLaw, 2026).
---
- **Supreme Court**
- Matter adjourned so parties can respond to the report (LiveLaw, 2026).
---
- **Start date**
- Targeted for the 2027 counselling cycle, subject to final regulations, court directions and consultation (Hindustan Times, 6 Nov 2026).
{% /table %}

An NMC official told Hindustan Times the commission will wait for the court's final directions before the next step, which is seeking stakeholder suggestions (Hindustan Times, 4 Nov 2026).

{% callout type="summary" %}
Recommended by the panel, approved in principle by NMC, not yet drafted, not yet notified, and still before the Supreme Court. Plan for it, but don't treat it as final.
{% /callout %}

## Why the panel wants it

It goes back to the cut-off cut of January 2026. NBEMS lowered the qualifying percentile for the third round of NEET PG 2025-26 counselling to zero for reserved categories, and from the 50th to the 7th percentile for general and EWS candidates, after thousands of seats stayed vacant. Petitioners challenged it in the Supreme Court (Hindustan Times, 4 Nov 2026).

The reported rationale is practical. The reforms aim to address vacant PG seats and counselling delays (Hindustan Times, 6 Nov 2026). The Centre also argued that NEET PG is meant to produce a merit list for allocating limited seats, not to certify minimum competence, which it says the MBBS degree already establishes (LiveLaw, 2026). A DGHS official said the changes should shorten counselling so sessions start on time (Hindustan Times, 4 Nov 2026).

The panel also said minimum standards shouldn't be diluted enough to admit candidates with "grossly inadequate or negative performance" (Hindustan Times, 4 Nov 2026).

## What the pushback is about

Two separate worries tend to get lumped together.

**Standards.** Petitioners and doctors' groups argue that a qualifying percentile is a minimum benchmark, and that changing the format doesn't answer the question of near-zero cut-offs (Hindustan Times, 6 Nov 2026).

**Score visibility.** One r/indianmedschool thread asks for raw marks, normalized marks and the normalization method to be published alongside any rank, so candidates can see how a result was calculated. The thread is about ranks plus normalized scores, so treat it as the student mood, not a reaction to a specific clause.

There's a gap here. The affidavit says no *percentile* marks will be declared. None of the reports we read says whether raw marks or scorecards will still be shown. That's a question for the draft regulations, and a fair one to raise in the consultation.

## How to judge your prep without a score

Whatever the final format, your mock results and your own tracking are the best readings you'll get. Here's how to use them.

{% actionBox title="A rank-proof prep check" %}
1. Track your rank in large mock pools over time. One bad rank is noise; a trend across several tests tells you something.
2. Turn rank into position yourself: your rank divided by the number of test-takers. It's simple arithmetic and works with or without a percentile.
3. Log accuracy by subject and topic after every test, not just a total.
4. Keep an error log: wrong because you didn't know it, misread it, or guessed. Each needs a different fix.
5. Put your weakest subjects on a spaced revision schedule and revisit them on fixed days.
6. Sit timed full-length tests regularly and review them the next day.
{% /actionBox %}

Ask whoever runs your mocks to show your raw score as well as your rank. If NEET PG only gives you a rank, you'll still have your own history to read it against.

## If you're an intern or final-year student

You may be reading this between postings. Start with the mock-test habits above, since they work whatever the final rule turns out to be. Beyond that, keep an eye on official sources. A draft is expected after stakeholder comments (LiveLaw, 2026), and what the rules say about marks and scorecards matters to everyone aiming for 2027.

## Frequently asked questions

### Is NEET PG 2027 officially rank-based?

Not yet. NMC approved the idea in principle, but the regulations are unfinished and the Supreme Court hasn't given final directions (Hindustan Times, 4 Nov 2026).

### Will my raw score be hidden?

The reports we read don't say. The affidavit says no percentile marks will be declared. Watch the draft regulations.

### Do the other panel recommendations affect me?

Possibly. The panel also suggested that state fee committees consider lower fees for pre-clinical and para-clinical courses, and reviews of stipends and bonds, but several of those are left to state governments (Hindustan Times, 6 Nov 2026).

## The short version

NEET PG may move to ranks only from the 2027 cycle, but for now it's a recommendation with NMC's in-principle approval, not a notified rule. The student concern about not seeing raw marks is fair and unresolved. What you can control is the same either way: a steady mock history, topic-level tracking, and honest revision of your weak subjects.

